Drama about Family in Live-Action
Directed By Briar March
Produced By Caroline Hutchison
Made In New Zealand
 Rob Munday
Sometimes it only takes a brief moment to change your life – one encounter or one small incident that suddenly shifts your perspective, for better or worse. For Julia, a mother struggling to accept that her baby has Down syndrome, that moment arrives during a simple trip to the store in Briar March’s life-affirming short film I See You.

Inspired by the personal experiences of March and her producer Caroline Hutchison, both mothers of children with Down syndrome, the film immerses us in Julia’s everyday reality as she grapples with feelings she isn’t proud of but can’t ignore. While the story is rooted in this specific experience, it also touches on something widely relatable: the quiet pressure many parents feel to compare their children to others, and the emotional weight that can come with that.

